DynamoDB table
  |
  v
One session record
  Example: contactid = "contact-1"
  |
  v
A map field inside that record
  Example: entities
  |
  v
One entry inside the map
  Example: TRANSFER_MONEY
  |
  v
Stored string values for that entry
  Example: t_entity_amount = "100"


DynamoDB table
  |
  v
{
  "contactid": "contact-1",        <- session record key

  "entities": {                    <- map field
    "TRANSFER_MONEY": {            <- entry inside the map
      "t_entity_amount": "100"     <- string value
    }
  }
}




In JSON form, the library writes this shape:

{
  "<partition key name>": "<partition key value>",
  "<map name>": {
    "<entry key>": {
      "<value name>": "<string value>"
    }
  }
}
Example:

{
  "contactid": "contact-1",
  "entities": {
    "TRANSFER_MONEY": {
      "t_entity_amount": "100",
      "t_entity_account": "checking"
    }
  }
}
In this example, contactid, entities, TRANSFER_MONEY, and the t_entity_* names are caller-owned conventions. The library only writes the nested map.

Responsibilities
The library owns:

Building DynamoDB UpdateItem requests.
Creating the top-level map attribute if it is missing.
Writing string values under one entry key.
Preventing accidental creation of a new session item.
Converting missing session items into ErrItemNotFound.
The caller owns:

Choosing the table name.
Choosing the partition key attribute name.
Choosing the partition key value for the session/contact.
Choosing the top-level map name.
Choosing the entry key.
Preparing the string values to store.
Applying any domain-specific naming rules.
